{
  "_doc": {
    "purpose": "Dependency-direction rules for src/, enforced by scripts/check-layering.ts. ADR-0001 chose rules over a folder reshape; this file is where the rules live. JSON has no comments, so the documentation is this key.",
    "edges": "An edge is \"<from>-><to>\" between top-level src/ domains. The domain of src/foo/bar.ts is \"foo\"; a file directly in src/ is \"(root)\". Non-test files only; self-edges are not modelled.",
    "frozenEdges": "A ratchet, not an endorsement. Every domain edge present when the gate landed is listed; an edge that is not listed fails the check. Adding one is a deliberate edit, which is the whole point. Removing the last import on an edge makes it prunable (a warning, never a failure).",
    "rules.applicationLayer": "ADR-0004: src/agents is the application layer, not a peer domain. Its outward edges are in-direction by definition and no rule constrains them. The rule that protects the boundary — peers not deep-importing agents — is carried by the frozen set, where today's inward edges are grandfathered.",
    "rules.portSurface": "ADR-0003: src/plugin-sdk is the published port surface. It must not import the gateway, the CLI or the command layer. This is a hard rule: it fails even for an edge that is listed in frozenEdges, so freezing cannot launder it. The three edges that already exist are named in rules.portSurface.grandfathered and warn instead — they are the only exceptions, so plugin-sdk cannot acquire a forbidden edge it does not already have.",
    "rules.leaf": "ADR-0005: src/shared is the helper home and should depend on nothing but itself and the src/ root. Mode is \"warn\" because it does not hold today — see the note field for the offending edges. Promote to \"fail\" when they are gone."
  },
